  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "aloha protein powder",
      "aloha organic plant based protein powder",
      "aloha protein powder review",
      "is aloha protein powder healthy",
      "aloha protein powder third party tested",
      "stevia free plant protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant-Based Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">ALOHA Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      ALOHA is best known for its organic protein bars, but it also sells a two-flavor plant protein powder with no stevia and no sugar alcohols. Here is what the label, the testing claims and the price actually show.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#sweetener">Sugar and Sweeteners</a>
      <a href="#vs">ALOHA vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#safety">Allergies and Pregnancy</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          ALOHA protein powder is a good organic plant protein for people who want to avoid stevia, but it is a light one: 18 g of protein per two-scoop serving, 130 calories and 4 g of added coconut sugar, per the label. At the Amazon price we saw, it costs about $2.13 per 25 g of protein. This review covers the label, testing, cost, taste reports and who should buy it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. ALOHA Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            ALOHA protein powder is a clean-label, organic plant protein that does one thing better than most rivals: it avoids stevia, sucralose and sugar alcohols entirely, per ALOHA. Each two-scoop serving gives 18 g of protein from organic pea, brown rice, hemp seed and pumpkin seed protein, with 130 calories and 4 g of added coconut sugar, per the label at the time of writing. At $22.99 for 15 servings on Amazon, it costs about $2.13 per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> stevia aftertaste ruins most plant shakes for you, you want USDA Organic and Non-GMO Project Verified on the tub, and 18 g of protein per shake is enough for your goals. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want 25 g or more per scoop, need a powder certified for tested sport (NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport), or are cutting added sugar to zero.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label fact</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chocolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Serving size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 level scoops (36 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 level scoops (35 g)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total / added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g / 4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g / 4 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">320 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g (Amazon, Nov 21, 2026)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.13</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.13</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. ALOHA Protein Powder and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            ALOHA sells both powder flavors on Amazon through its own storefront (seller ALOHA_MOMENT), so both are below with the prices we saw on November 21, 2026, followed by three organic plant proteins that give more protein per scoop or cost less per gram.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in ALOHA Organic Plant-Based Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            ALOHA protein powder contains an organic protein blend of pea, brown rice, hemp seed and pumpkin seed protein, followed by flavoring (cocoa or ground vanilla beans), coconut sugar, blue agave inulin, xanthan gum, monk fruit extract, MCT oil from coconut and cinnamon, per the printed ingredient list. Everything except the xanthan gum and the sea salt is marked organic. ALOHA does not publish the ratio of the four proteins, so you cannot tell how much of the 18 g comes from pea versus the smaller hemp and pumpkin contributions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The chocolate panel, per the label at the time of writing, reads: 36 g serving, 130 calories, 3 g of fat (0 g saturated), 320 mg of sodium, 9 g of carbohydrate, 5 g of fiber, 4 g of total sugars (all 4 g added) and 18 g of protein. It also lists 63 mg of calcium, 7 mg of iron (40% of the daily value) and 274 mg of potassium. The vanilla panel is close: 35 g, 130 calories, 2.5 g of fat, 250 mg of sodium, 10 g of carbohydrate, 5 g of fiber, 5 g of total sugars with 4 g added, 18 g of protein and 6 mg of iron. Both panels say "About 15 servings per container."
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein density is the weak spot. Eighteen grams in a 36 g scoop means the powder is about 50% protein by weight, and protein supplies roughly 55% of the calories. A typical pea-rice blend such as <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani</a> reaches 20 g in 33 g, and sport-style plant powders such as <a href="/owyn-protein-powder-review/">OWYN</a> reach 30 g. The difference goes to fiber (blue agave inulin), coconut sugar, cocoa and MCT oil: ingredients that make the shake taste and feel more like food, at the cost of protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fiber is a real plus for some people and a problem for others. Five grams per serving, most of it from agave inulin, is 18% of the daily value and helps a shake keep you full. Inulin is also a fermentable fiber, so people who bloat on chicory root or onion-type fibers may notice it. If that is you, start with one scoop instead of two and see how it sits.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (Chocolate, per label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 36 g serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">130</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fiber</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The certifications printed on the panel are USDA Organic, the Orthodox Union Kosher symbol, the Vegan.org Certified Vegan seal and Non-GMO Project Verified. ALOHA's product page adds that the powder is gluten-free, made with Fair Trade Certified ingredients and made by a Certified B Corporation. None of those are sport-testing certifications, which is the subject of the next section. For the full numbers in a sortable format, see our <a href="/protein-powder/">protein powder label database</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is ALOHA Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, partly: ALOHA says each product is tested by an independent third-party lab for lead, cadmium, mercury and arsenic, and that every finished batch is tested to under 5 ppm gluten, per its FAQ. What ALOHA does not have is a sport certification. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing for the powder, and ALOHA does not claim one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The heavy-metals claim is worth reading closely. ALOHA's FAQ answers the question "Are ALOHA bars tested for heavy metals?" by saying it "carefully test[s] each product using an independent 3rd party provider" for the four metals, and that its levels are "among the lowest found in similar products on the market." That is a brand statement. ALOHA does not publish the lab results, lot certificates or the limits it tests against, so a buyer cannot check the numbers the way they can with brands that post a certificate of analysis for each lot, such as <a href="/ora-protein-powder-review/">Ora Organic</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gluten testing is more concrete. ALOHA says it does not own its manufacturing plants, so it cannot promise a line has never run wheat; instead, every finished batch is independently tested to under 5 ppm gluten, which is a stricter limit than the 20 ppm used by the Certified Gluten-Free standard. The powder is not itself carrying a Certified Gluten-Free seal, but the testing claim is specific and the limit is stated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why does the sport certification matter? NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test for banned substances and check that the label matches the contents, lot by lot. That matters to drug-tested athletes and to anyone who wants an outside party, not the brand, standing behind the label. For most people drinking ALOHA as a breakfast shake, the organic certification and the brand's metals testing will be enough. If you compete under drug testing, choose a powder from our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF Certified protein powder</a> list instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Organic is not the same as tested for contaminants.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">USDA Organic certifies how ingredients are grown and handled. It says nothing about lead or banned substances. Plant proteins absorb metals from soil, so ask for published results, not just a seal. Our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that publish theirs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

