          <p>
            The most detailed independent write-up we found, a 2024 review on The Gadgeteer, tried the vanilla with 12 ounces of water. The reviewer said it blended smoothly in a shaker bottle with no grit, which they noted is unusual for a pea-based shake, and that it was not overly sweet. The complaints were that the result was "a thin, watery shake" and that a slight coconut flavor lingered. The reviewer guessed the chocolate would suit them better and planned to add cocoa to mask the coconut.
          </p>

          <p>
            That coconut note has an obvious source on the label: MCT oil from coconut and coconut sugar are in both flavors. ALOHA itself describes the chocolate as rich and velvety with a hint of coconut sugar and cinnamon, and the vanilla as subtly sweet, and suggests mixing with almond or oat milk for a creamier shake. Brand copy is not independent evidence, but the advice matches what the reviewer found: in water, a 35 g serving with modest sweetness is going to taste light.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to get a better ALOHA shake</h3>

          <p>
            Based on the label and the reports above, three things help. Use milk or a creamy plant milk instead of water, since the powder contains no creamer base or thickening beyond a little xanthan gum. Blend it with frozen banana or oats rather than shaking it, which hides the thinness. And if you dislike coconut, pick chocolate, where the cocoa and cinnamon cover more of it. People who want a thick, dessert-like shake out of water alone usually do better with a whey or a creamer-based plant powder; our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best-tasting protein powder</a> guide covers those.
          </p>

          <p>
            The low sweetness is a feature for some buyers. Many plant powders compensate for earthy pea flavor with heavy stevia, which leaves a bitter or licorice finish that a large share of people dislike. ALOHA's coconut sugar and small amount of monk fruit give a milder, more food-like sweetness. If you have given up on plant shakes because of stevia, that difference may matter more than the texture.
          </p>
