        <div id="sweetener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does ALOHA Protein Powder Have Stevia, Sugar or Sugar Alcohols?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No stevia and no sugar alcohols: ALOHA protein powder is sweetened with organic coconut sugar and organic monk fruit extract, and ALOHA says it never uses stevia, sucralose, aspartame, erythritol, maltitol, xylitol or sorbitol. It does contain 4 g of added sugar per serving from the coconut sugar, per the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Four grams of added sugar is 8% of the daily value, about one teaspoon. For most people that is a trivial amount in a 130-calorie shake, and it is less than a typical flavored yogurt. It does mean the powder is not a zero-sugar product, so people managing blood sugar or following a strict low-sugar plan should count it. Coconut sugar is still sugar; its marketing as a "lower glycemic" sweetener does not change the gram count on the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The monk fruit matters for a specific reason. Many products sold as monk fruit sweetened are mostly erythritol, because pure monk fruit extract is intensely sweet and hard to dose. ALOHA's FAQ states that the monk fruit it uses contains no erythritol. That is a meaningful detail for anyone avoiding sugar alcohols for digestive reasons, and it puts ALOHA in a small group alongside Truvani and Ora that sweeten without stevia, sucralose or erythritol.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the formula is similarly restrained: no artificial colors, no artificial flavors (the natural flavor is marked organic), no soy and no dairy ingredients. The one additive worth naming is xanthan gum, a common thickener that is generally well tolerated in small amounts. For more options built around the same idea, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> guide.
          </p>
