        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is ALOHA Protein Powder Safe for Allergies, Kids and Pregnancy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            ALOHA protein powder is free of dairy, soy and gluten ingredients, but it contains coconut and is made in a facility that also processes wheat, eggs, milk, fish, tree nuts (almonds) and soy, per the label. People with severe allergies should weigh that shared-facility statement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coconut is the allergen to note. The FDA removed coconut from its list of tree nuts in January 2025, which ALOHA acknowledges in its FAQ, but some people with tree-nut allergies still avoid it, and coconut allergy exists in its own right. ALOHA's FAQ also describes a slightly broader facility statement for its bars and powders, adding peanuts and sesame, so if you react to trace amounts, read the current label on your canister and ask ALOHA directly (it lists Care@aloha.com for questions).
          </p>

          <p>
            For children, ALOHA says its products are safe for kids, and the ingredients are ordinary foods: plant proteins, cocoa, coconut sugar and fiber. Most children who eat a normal diet get enough protein from food, so a protein shake is a convenience, not a need. If you are considering a protein powder for a child with a medical condition, a growth concern or a restrictive diet, talk to a pediatrician or registered dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            For pregnancy and breastfeeding, ALOHA's FAQ says its products are made with natural, shelf-stable ingredients but tells anyone with concerns to consult a medical professional. That is the right advice. An organic, stevia-free plant powder with no herbal extracts is a reasonable candidate, but because ALOHA does not publish its heavy-metal results, ask your doctor or midwife and see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pregnancy/">protein powder for pregnancy</a> guide for options with published testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same caution applies to anyone with kidney disease, anyone on a protein-restricted diet and older adults managing a medical condition: check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein shake. The 5 g of fiber per serving is also worth knowing if you have a sensitive gut or have been told to limit fermentable fibers such as inulin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy ALOHA Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            ALOHA protein powder is best for people who want an organic, stevia-free plant shake as a light breakfast or snack and do not need a high protein dose. It is a poor fit for lifters chasing 25 to 30 g per scoop, for drug-tested athletes and for anyone cutting added sugar to zero.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy ALOHA if</h3>

          <p>
            You have tried several plant powders and quit because of the stevia aftertaste. You want USDA Organic, Non-GMO Project Verified, certified vegan and Kosher on the same tub. You like a little fiber in your shake to stay full, and you usually blend protein into smoothies or oats rather than drinking it with water. You already like ALOHA bars and want the same ingredient philosophy in a powder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip ALOHA if</h3>

          <p>
            You want the most protein per dollar: Orgain is cheaper per gram, and conventional whey costs less than half as much. You want 25 g or more per scoop without using three scoops; look at the higher-protein options in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> roundup or our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein</a> guide. You compete in a drug-tested sport and need NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Or you want published heavy-metal results rather than a brand statement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our overall verdict: ALOHA's powder is honest and well made for what it is, a gentle organic shake with a short ingredient list and a clean sweetener story. It is not a muscle-building powder, and at about $2.13 per 25 g it is not a bargain. Buy it on Amazon rather than direct, start with chocolate, and mix it with milk.
          </p>
